## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures, promise deduping, in-flight concurrency control, and simple caching in a realistic data-loading scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Closure-scoped internal state (inFlight, cache, queue)
- Promise coalescing for same id
- Concurrency limit with a slot-based throttle
- Time-based cache with TTL

## Code Example
```javascript
function createUserLoader() {
  const inFlight = new Map(); // id -> Promise
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> { data, expiry }
  const queue = [];
  const MAX_CONCURRENCY = 3;
  let active = 0;

  const fetchUser = async (id) => {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/user/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Network error');
    return res.json();
  };

  const tryStart = () => {
    while (active < MAX_CONCURRENCY && queue.length) {
      const { id, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      active++;
      const p = fetchUser(id)
        .then((data) => {
          cache.set(id, { data, expiry: Date.now() + 5 * 60 * 1000 });
          inFlight.delete(id);
          resolve(data);
        })
        .catch((err) => reject(err))
        .finally(() => { active--; tryStart(); });
      inFlight.set(id, p);
    }
  };

  const loadUser = (id) => {
    const cached = cache.get(id);
    if (cached && cached.expiry > Date.now()) return Promise.resolve(cached.data);
    if (inFlight.has(id)) return inFlight.get(id);
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ id, resolve, reject });
      tryStart();
    });
  };

  return { loadUser };
}

// Usage
// const loader = createUserLoader();
// loader.loadUser('123').then(console.log);
```

## Solution Overview
Use closure to encapsulate rate limiter state with request queue and timer-based enforcement.

## Key Components
- Queue array for pending requests
- Processing flag to prevent race conditions
- Promise chaining for FIFO ordering
- Timer-based rate limit enforcement

## Implementation
```javascript
const createRateLimiter = (limit, interval) => {
  const queue = [];
  let processing = false;
  let lastRequest = 0;

  return async (request) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ request, resolve, reject });
      processQueue();
    });
  };

  async function processQueue() {
    if (processing || queue.length === 0) return;
    processing = true;

    const now = Date.now();
    const timeSinceLastRequest = now - lastRequest;
    const delay = timeSinceLastRequest < interval ? interval - timeSinceLastRequest : 0;

    setTimeout(async () => {
      const { request, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      lastRequest = Date.now();

      try {
        const result = await request();
        resolve(result);
      } catch (error) {
        reject(error);
      }

      processing = false;
      processQueue();
    }, delay);
  }
};
```

## Benefits
- Maintains request order through FIFO queue
- Prevents rate limit violations with timing control
- Handles concurrent requests gracefully

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures, promise sharing, and TTL eviction in a small utility.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and per-id state
- Promise deduplication for in-flight calls
- TTL-based eviction and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function createMemoizedAsync(fn, ttl) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return async (id) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    const entry = cache.get(id);
    if (entry && now - entry.t < ttl) return entry.p;
    const p = fn(id);
    cache.set(id, { t: now, p });
    setTimeout(() => {
      const e = cache.get(id);
      if (e && e.p === p) cache.delete(id);
    }, ttl);
    try {
      return await p;
    } catch (e) {
      cache.delete(id);
      throw e;
    }
  };
}
```

